From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MIRAGE <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 13:07:16 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] MIRAGE: a write copies only its path; no Nagle; grep, which
 and relative links

What the fingerprint suite's second run still found:

- A session's first write deep-copied the whole filesystem tree: 137 ms for a
  tree built from a real image. A private tree now starts as a copy of the
  root alone, and getfile() copies each node on the way to the one it hands
  out, so a session owns exactly the nodes it can change.
- Every command answered in several writes waited 40 ms for a delayed ACK.
  TCP_NODELAY is now set when the connection is made, not only with a pty.
- grep ignored -c, -v, -n, -l, -o, -w, -x, -r, -E and -F, did not name files
  and read a basic expression as an extended one. It is rewritten, with
  egrep and fgrep.
- which printed every place along PATH; Debian's prints the first.
- A relative symbolic link was read from the root whatever directory held
  it. It is read from its own directory, and a loop ends as ELOOP does.
